poutil-unix

Strict POSIX utilities in Rust. One small program per utility, not a
multicall binary. Written against the SUSv4 (POSIX.1-2008/2016) utility
pages and checked against the VSC-PCTS conformance suite, the native
tools of the host system and GNU coreutils.


ABOUT THIS PROJECT

This is a passion project. I build it for the love of Unix and to learn
the POSIX utilities from the inside, one specification page at a time.
It moves when I have the time and energy: there is no roadmap, no release
schedule and no promise of support. Being a hobby also means I can afford
to be picky, so strictness wins over convenience here.

It is not a drop-in replacement for the tools of your system, so do not
put it ahead of /bin on a machine you care about. Passing the tests is
also not POSIX certification: this project is not certified, and "POSIX"
is a trademark of the IEEE.


USE OF AI

I wrote this project with Claude Code, Anthropic's AI coding agent. Much
of the source, the tests and the documentation (this README included)
was written by Claude Code under my direction. The decisions are mine:
Rust with only libc, strict POSIX, bytes instead of UTF-8, one binary
per utility, the layout, and what is left out. I run the privileged
steps myself, and a utility only counts as done once it passes its unit
tests, the differential tests and the VSC-PCTS. Commits made with its
help carry an Assisted-by trailer.


STATUS

Implemented, unit tested, difftested and PCTS clean on my machine:

	true false echo basename dirname pwd sleep logname tty uname
	unlink link cat tee rmdir mkdir mkfifo pathchk env nohup nice
	time kill

More are planned (expand, unexpand, wc, comm, cmp, head, tsort, and
onwards through the stream filters, file tools and so on). Deliberately
left out: sh and the shell builtins, job control, terminal and daemon
tools. getconf is postponed because of its large per-platform name
tables.

Only tested on x86_64 macOS so far. The code needs nothing but libc and
has a few cfg(apple) spots (environ, errno, signal names), so Linux and
the BSDs will need small fixes. Patches welcome.


DESIGN RULES

Strict POSIX. Extensions are rejected, so for example "sleep 1.5" is an
error, not a feature.

Bytes, not UTF-8. Arguments and file names are handled as byte strings;
String and std::env::args() are never used for user data.

The only dependency is the libc crate. No clap, no regex crate: grep,
sed, awk and expr will use regcomp and regexec from libc.

Every utility is a pure function core that can be unit tested plus a
thin main(). Utilities that talk to the outside world call posix::init()
first, which restores signal and descriptor state that the Rust runtime
changes before main() runs. true and false are so small that they do not
need it and do not use the shared library at all.

Comments are K&R style (slash-star), never double-slash.


BUILDING

You need a Rust toolchain (cargo).

	make            build all utilities into target/release
	make test       run the unit tests and the process-level checks
	make clean

Layout is Unix style rather than a crate tree: one directory per utility
(cat/cat.rs), shared code in libposix/, a single Cargo.toml with one bin
entry per utility, and a Makefile wrapper.


TESTING

Unit tests (cargo test) cover the pure cores: option parsing, the mode
grammar, path splitting, escape expansion and so on. Process-level checks
(sh difftest/regress.sh) cover what only a real process shows: redirections
such as cat f >> f, closed descriptors, SIGPIPE, descriptor limits, signals
sent to real processes, umask and inherited signal dispositions. They run
under a file size limit so that a runaway writer cannot fill the disk.
"make test" runs both.

Differential tests compare our output, exit status and side effects with
the native tools (/usr/bin) and GNU coreutils (the g-prefixed ones, for
example from MacPorts: /opt/local/bin/gcat), in the C locale and in
en_US.UTF-8:

	make && sh difftest/difftest.sh echo basename dirname cat

Test cases live in difftest/cases/UTILITY. A line containing only @ means "no
arguments". Differences judged acceptable are listed in UTILITY.known
with the reason in a comment. Optional files: UTILITY.setup (run before),
UTILITY.stdin (instead of /dev/null) and UTILITY.show (run afterwards,
its output is compared too). The specification decides who is right;
macOS and GNU both deviate from it in places.

VSC-PCTS, the POSIX conformance suite from The Open Group, is not
included and cannot be redistributed, so the scripts I use to
drive it (macOS, a dedicated test user, a root-owned sudo wrapper) are
not part of this repository either.

One piece is kept: "make deploy" installs the built utilities, root-owned,
into the directory that the suite runs put ahead of the system tools in
PATH. It asks for sudo on purpose. Override VSC_USER, VSC_GROUP or OURS on
the make command line for a different setup.


CONTRIBUTING

Bug reports and patches are welcome, with the caveat above that I answer
when I can. If you used an AI tool for a patch, say so in the commit
message with an Assisted-by trailer, as I do.

Commit messages follow the usual Unix style: a subject of about 50
characters in the imperative, prefixed with the directory it touches,
then a blank line and a body wrapped at 72 columns that says what changed
and why. One logical change per commit.

	pathchk: reject an empty pathname only with -P

	The PCTS expects this and macOS agrees. GNU rejects it always;
	the difference is recorded in difftest/cases/pathchk.known.

	Assisted-by: Claude Code


